A closer candidate is the paper’s Section 3 \(K_4\) dichotomy: one 6-distance tetrahedron type is detected by XOR \(=0\). A simple “map this one tetrahedron” task fails the hardness test, though—the certificate is recovered by linear-time column matching in about the same number of operations as the compact route. I’m therefore checking the authors’ actual cover/coloring artifacts before deciding whether there is a defensible planted-coloring distribution or whether the honest outcome is rejection.
